{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seokjin","familyName":"Go","name":"Go, Seokjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joongun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Joongun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Spandan","familyName":"More","name":"More, Spandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanjiang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hanjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irene","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Irene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Jezghani","name":"Jezghani, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tushar","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Tushar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divya","familyName":"Mahajan","name":"Mahajan, Divya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Characterizing the Efficiency of Distributed Training: A Power, Performance, and Thermal Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:05:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:43:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T14:28:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:50:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid scaling of Large Language Models (LLMs) has pushed training workloads far beyond the limits of single-node analysis, demanding a deeper understanding of how these models behave across large-scale, multi-GPU systems. In this paper, we present a comprehensive characterization of LLM training across diverse real-world workloads and hardware platforms, including NVIDIA H100/H200 and AMD MI250 GPUs. We analyze dense and sparse models under various parallelism strategies -- tensor, pipeline, data, and expert -- and evaluate their effects on hardware utilization, power consumption, and thermal behavior. We further evaluate the effectiveness of optimizations such as activation recomputation and compute-communication overlap. Our findings show that performance is not determined solely by scaling hardware capacity. Scale-up systems with fewer, higher-memory GPUs can outperform scale-out systems in communication-bound regimes, but only under carefully tuned configurations; in other cases, scale-out deployments achieve superior throughput. We also show that certain parallelism combinations, such as tensor with pipeline, lead to bandwidth underutilization due to inefficient data chunking, while increasing microbatch sizes beyond a certain point induces bursty execution and peak power excursions that worsen thermal throttling. These insights reveal how training performance is shaped by complex interactions between hardware, system topology, and model execution. We conclude by offering recommendations for system and hardware design to improve the scalability and reliability of future LLM systems and workloads. The source code of this project is available at https://github.com/sitar-lab/CharLLM-PPT."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:33:26Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:33:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:48:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10613","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10613","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10613","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniil","familyName":"Shmelev","name":"Shmelev, Daniil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristopher","familyName":"Salvi","name":"Salvi, Cristopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"pySigLib -- Fast Signature-Based Computations on CPU and GPU"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Software (cs.MS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60L10, 65Y05, 68T99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:00:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:59:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Signature-based methods have recently gained significant traction in machine learning for sequential data. In particular, signature kernels have emerged as powerful discriminators and training losses for generative models on time-series, notably in quantitative finance. However, existing implementations do not scale to the dataset sizes and sequence lengths encountered in practice. We present pySigLib, a high-performance Python library offering optimised implementations of signatures and signature kernels on CPU and GPU, fully compatible with PyTorch's automatic differentiation. Beyond an efficient software stack for large-scale signature-based computation, we introduce a novel differentiation scheme for signature kernels that delivers accurate gradients at a fraction of the runtime of existing libraries."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10613","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:43:21Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:43:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10369","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10369","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10369","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gul Rukh","familyName":"Khattak","name":"Khattak, Gul Rukh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Patlatzoglou","name":"Patlatzoglou, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Barker","name":"Barker, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libor","familyName":"Pastika","name":"Pastika, Libor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boroumand","familyName":"Zeidaabadi","name":"Zeidaabadi, Boroumand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"El-Medany","name":"El-Medany, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hesham","familyName":"Aggour","name":"Aggour, Hesham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiu","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yixiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio H.","familyName":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Antonio H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Annis","name":"Annis, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio Luiz Pinho","familyName":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Antonio Luiz Pinho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junbo","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Junbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel B.","familyName":"Kramer","name":"Kramer, Daniel B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan W.","familyName":"Waks","name":"Waks, Jonathan W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Brittain","name":"Brittain, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Peters","name":"Peters, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fu Siong","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Fu Siong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arunashis","familyName":"Sau","name":"Sau, Arunashis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data distribution impacts the performance and generalisability of contrastive learning-based foundation models of electrocardiograms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Tissues and Organs (q-bio.TO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:01:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:42:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contrastive learning is a widely adopted self-supervised pretraining strategy, yet its dependence on cohort composition remains underexplored. We present Contrasting by Patient Augmented Electrocardiograms (CAPE) foundation model and pretrain on four cohorts (n = 5,203,352), from diverse populations across three continents (North America, South America, Asia). We systematically assess how cohort demographics, health status, and population diversity influence the downstream performance for prediction tasks also including two additional cohorts from another continent (Europe). We find that downstream performance depends on the distributional properties of the pretraining cohort, including demographics and health status. Moreover, while pretraining with a multi-centre, demographically diverse cohort improves in-distribution accuracy, it reduces out-of-distribution (OOD) generalisation of our contrastive approach by encoding cohort-specific artifacts. To address this, we propose the In-Distribution Batch (IDB) strategy, which preserves intra-cohort consistency during pretraining and enhances OOD robustness. This work provides important insights for developing clinically fair and generalisable foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Currently under review at npj Digital Medicine"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10369","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:33:23Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:33:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:33:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":3,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10369+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10371+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10613"}}